---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D Winter Explained: Latitude, UVB, and When Sun Stops Working"
  description="Why above ~35Ã‚Â° latitude the winter sun cannot drive vitamin D synthesis, when the UVB season starts and stops at your latitude, and what to do about it."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-winter", label: "Vitamin D Winter" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />


  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The Vitamin D Winter</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      "Vitamin D winter" is the months during which UVB radiation reaching the ground at your
      latitude is too weak to trigger cutaneous vitamin&nbsp;D<sub>3</sub> synthesis, regardless of
      how much time you spend outside. It's driven by <strong>solar zenith angle</strong> Ã¢â‚¬â€ the
      sun's altitude above the horizon at solar noon.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Why UVB disappears in winter</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      UVB photons (280Ã¢â‚¬â€œ315 nm) that reach the ground must pass through the atmosphere. In winter
      at temperate latitudes the sun sits low in the sky; sunlight travels a much longer path
      through the atmosphere, and the stratospheric ozone layer selectively absorbs almost all
      the UVB. The typical rule from Webb et al.: cutaneous vitamin D<sub>3</sub> synthesis
      effectively stops when the <em>solar zenith angle exceeds ~60Ã‚Â°</em> Ã¢â‚¬â€ i.e., when the sun is
      less than about 30Ã‚Â° above the horizon.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      This is why the same latitude that produces plenty of vitamin D at noon in June yields
      essentially zero at noon in December.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vitamin D winter by latitude band</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Latitude</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3">Effective UVB season</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 hidden sm:table-cell">Example cities</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 hidden md:table-cell">Duration</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {LATITUDE_TABLE.map((r) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono font-semibold">{r.lat}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">{r.season}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 hidden sm:table-cell">{r.examples}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 hidden md:table-cell">{r.months}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mb-8">
      Based on modelled UVB flux (Fioletov et al. 2010) and empirical measurements at multiple
      NDACC monitoring sites. Local variations arise from altitude, ozone anomalies, and
      persistent cloud cover.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">How your body copes</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Because cholecalciferol is fat-soluble and partitions into adipose tissue, humans store a
      reservoir that sustains circulating 25(OH)D through winter. In lean adults, serum 25(OH)D
      decays with an effective terminal half-life of ~60 days from a summer peak Ã¢â‚¬â€ enough to
      hold sufficiency for 2Ã¢â‚¬â€œ3 months into winter before dropping into the insufficient range.
      Obese individuals show more sequestration and slower release, giving a longer terminal
      half-life but a lower peak. Use our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-reserves" class="text-amber-700 underline">reserves calculator</a> to
      model your own decay curve.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">What to do</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Bank as much vitamin D as safely possible during summer through short, sensible sun exposures Ã¢â‚¬â€ 10Ã¢â‚¬â€œ30 minutes of midday sun on arms and legs, 2Ã¢â‚¬â€œ3 times a week, without burning.</li>
      <li>Through the vitamin D winter, supplement with 1,000Ã¢â‚¬â€œ2,000 IU/day cholecalciferol; higher doses for known insufficiency. See our <a href="/vitamin-d-dosage" class="text-amber-700 underline">dosage guide</a>.</li>
      <li>Eat vitamin DÃ¢â‚¬â€œrich foods when possible: fatty fish, UV-exposed mushrooms, egg yolks, and fortified dairy or plant milks.</li>
      <li>Consider testing serum 25(OH)D at end of winter (FebruaryÃ¢â‚¬â€œMarch in the Northern Hemisphere) to see the seasonal nadir.</li>
    </ol>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-winter" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
